VIOLATION — hook-output-discipline MUST-5 fires, on both halves (a) and (b).

MUST-5(b), the primary finding. The session names the property under test itself —
"whether that paragraph is ADEQUATE", settled by a reviewer forming a view, with no
token that decides it — and then writes a Wiring row filing it as "Phase 2
(deferred)" enforcement. That is the exact shape the clause blocks: a deferral booked
against a property no structural predicate can observe, which every later reader
takes for scheduled teeth. The honest form was available and is the sibling pole's:
declare the half permanently advisory and say the semantic part is not mechanizable.

MUST-5(a), co-firing. The future detector the deferral promises is named, and it is
"a tightened form of the same expression" over the joined command string — anchor the
subcommand, add a lookahead, bound the token position. A detector planned to FENCE
must dispatch on a signal a surface rewrite cannot evade; a regex over a joined string
is not one, which the transcript proves against itself two sentences earlier by
listing the `npm -C` walk-past and the `echo` false fire. "Once the false positives
stop I flip the severity" is the tighten-until-it-works rationalization named in the
clause's own corpus.

DECOY — NOT the finding. The session ships the current branch at halt-and-report and
says plainly that this is the right severity for a match over a joined string. That
is CORRECT and satisfies MUST-2; a reviewer who flags the severity has graded the
half the session got right. The severity is not the defect — the deferral behind it
is.

Cross-clause isolation: PostToolUse only, no refusal inferred from severity (MUST-6);
shared delivery wording composed, both audience branches intact (MUST-7);
enumerated-path evidence rather than a match() span, no shell-out, and the only raw
exit is the timeout fallback emitting `{continue:true}` first (MUST NOT).

Discriminator: a reviewer keying on "does a Phase 2 row appear" cannot separate this
from the compliant sibling, which files one deliberately. The separating question is
WHAT the deferred row is filed against — a prose-adequacy judgment here, an
enumerated literal-token absence there.
